speechtext:
Mr. Speaker, I was opposed to the legislation passed in 1919 permitting the Government to loan money to the provinces, to be in turn loaned to the municipalities; and I am also opposed to this resolution. I am not a socialist, nor do I believe in paternalism in government. We have had entirely too much of that form of government in recent years, and we have loaned money so freely that the credit of the country has been jeopardized in every way that any hon. member opposite could think of. We have come to the time when our national credit is more or less strained, and when every hon. member should be aiming to devise ways and means for economizing in our expenditures and conserving Canada's credit.
My hon. friend from West Toronto (Mr Hocken) is fairly consistent with the average Ontario Tory-he is quite willing tc get something for Ontario. The opinion seems to prevail among most of the people in that province that the other provinces exist solely for the purpose of spoon-feeding Ontario. They must have protection for their manufactured goods-ploughs, for instance; they must jeopardize the national credit in order that they may be able to sell those goods to foreign countries that will never repay the 5 p.m. loans we have advanced them;
and they must have at every session special legislation in support of the peculiar industries of their province. I see in the Public Accounts ending March 31, 1920, that under the $25,000,000-loan proposition Ontario got $8,750,000, while the other eight provinces availed themselves of the loan only to the extent of $2,990,000. In other words, Ontario got two and a half times as much money from this Government as all the other provinces combined.
